MCSKIP MT,<>
MCINS %.
A macro label is remembered as it is passed, so a jump can go back to
a label already seen; one not yet seen is found by a forward search
from the point of scan. Both work inside a replacement text, so a loop
there may jump back or recurse. Only the source text is restricted: it
cannot jump backwards, so a loop must be moved into a macro.
MCDEF COUNTDOWN; AS <MCGO L1 IF %A1. GR 0
liftoff<>MCGO L0
%L1.%A1. COUNTDOWN %%A1.-1.;>
COUNTDOWN 3;
MCDEF FACT; AS <MCGO L1 IF %A1. GR 1
1<>MCGO L0
%L1.%%A1.*FACT %%A1.-1.;.>
FACT 5; should be 120
FACT 1; should be 1
This one loops instead, jumping back to a label it has already passed.
The counter has to be a permanent variable, because an argument does
not change between iterations, and the jump is taken while the test
fails rather than while it holds.
MCDEF COUNTUP; AS <MCSET P1=1
%L1.[%P1.]MCSET P1=P1+1
MCGO L1 UNLESS P1 GR %A1.
>
COUNTUP 5;
